WORKPLACE SAFETY INCIDENT REPORTING MANUAL

Chapter 1: Purpose and Scope
This manual covers reporting procedures for all workplace safety incidents oc=
curring on company premises or during company activities.

Chapter 2: Incident Classification
- Minor: First aid only, no lost work time (e.g., small cuts, bruises)
- Moderate: Medical treatment needed, less than 3 lost work days
- Serious: Hospitalization, more than 3 lost work days, or restricted duty

Chapter 3: Internal Reporting
All incidents must be logged in the safety tracker. The following notificatio=
ns are required:
- Minor: Email the employee's manager. Update Status to 'Documented'.
- Moderate: Email the Safety Officer (safety-officer@company.example.com) AND=
 the employee's manager. Update Status to 'Under Review'.
- Serious: Email the Safety Officer, the employee's manager, AND the VP Opera=
tions (vp-ops@company.example.com). Update Status to 'Investigation'.

Chapter 4: Workers' Compensation
All moderate and serious incidents require a workers' comp claim form. This i=
s handled by the benefits team separately.

Chapter 5: OSHA Reporting
IMPORTANT: Any incident resulting in hospitalization, amputation, or loss of =
an eye must be reported to OSHA within 24 hours. Email the Legal team (legal@=
company.example.com) immediately for OSHA-reportable incidents. These take pr=
ecedence over all other routing.

Chapter 6: Return to Work
Employees returning from injury leave must complete a return-to-work assessme=
nt.

Chapter 7: Record Retention
All safety records must be retained for 5 years.

